		<comps>
			<li Class="MVCF.Comps.CompProperties_VerbProps">
				<compClass>MVCF.Comps.Comp_VerbProps</compClass>
				<verbProps>
					<li>
						<label>M16</label>
						<canBeToggled>true</canBeToggled>
						<separateToggle>false</separateToggle>
					</li>
					<li>
						<label>Triple 40mm</label>
						<canBeToggled>true</canBeToggled>
						<separateToggle>false</separateToggle>
						<description>Caliber: 40x46mm. 3-round module. Triple barrel.
The "Triple 40mm" was an experimental underbarrel grenade launcher developed by the Naval Weapons Center (NWC) at China Lake for use with the M16 platform during the Vietnam War. Unlike the better-known China Lake pump-action grenade launcher, this design used three short 40mm barrels mounted together beneath the rifle, giving the operator three immediate shots without needing to reload after each round. Only a handful of prototypes were produced, and at least some were reportedly tested by special operations units in Vietnam. Although compact in concept, the launcher was wide, heavy, awkwardly balanced, and vulnerable to mud and debris entering the exposed barrels. It was not adopted, and the simpler M203 became the dominant underbarrel grenade launcher for the M16.</description>
					</li>
				</verbProps>
			</li>
